I'm shocked that this has happened now  and so soon you know I talked to a lot  of people who are close to Twitter I've  talked to some of the founders at times  and all of them have been very publicly  supportive of Dick Costolo as CEO Dick  Costolo has told me time and again that  he and the board are aligned he will be  the CEO of this company he feels that he  has the support of evan williams and  Jack Dorsey who founded this company and  now we're hearing that Chuck Dorsey is  coming in as interim CEO this is a guy  who's currently running another  multi-billion dollar company square you  know that said there has been a lot of  momentum behind investors who are just  not happy with Twitter's performance you  and I were just talking about Chris  Sacca a few minutes ago who released an  8,500 word missive about what he thinks  that Twitter can do to be better I asked  him many many times if he thinks that  dick costolo should still be the CEO and  he was publicly in that moment  supportive of Dick Costolo he felt that  dick costolo could get some of these  things done that he's asking to be done  but the reality is 1 billion people have  have come to Twitter have tried Twitter  and they haven't stuck around this is a  company that's suffering lagging user  growth lagging revenue this is a company  that so many people including myself  think can be bigger than than it already  is there's so much that goes on on  Twitter live tweets from basketball  players in the middle of a game you know  periscoping behind the scenes but that  kind of stuff is too difficult for  regular people who don't understand that  service to find Twitter actually takes a  lot of work and these are some things  that dick costolo hasn't been able to  fix since he's been the CEO there's been  a lot of turnover five product heads in  five years there was a lot of talk that  it certainly seemed like dick had  assembled a team the team that could  finally execute on these things he  brought in Anthony noto the new CFO he  brought in and promoted a new product  guy named Kevin will and it certainly  seemed like things were getting in order  but it doesn't seem like the board has  confidence in him at this  point and Emily I don't obviously want  you to speculate on what triggered this  right now but in your experience who had  been pushing for some kind of change at  the top and how quickly was that voice  actually growing because Twitter is  still affirming it's a second quarter  guidance it's not like all of a sudden  things that magically changed so first  of all Wall Street Wall Street has been  pretty hard on Dick Costolo I mean there  have been people out there time and  again saying the CEO of Twitter should  be fired so there was a lot of pressure  from the outside people who were not  within the company saying that this  company can be more that it can be I  mean over the years there has been so  much drama with regard to this company  Nick Bilton wrote an entire book on it  and you know as far as I can tell in  many situations it seemed that the board  has not necessarily been a line there  been a lot of people rotating into the  board and rotating out of the board  there's a lot of a lot of bad blood on  the floor quite frankly with how you  know Evan Williams was a CEO then Jack  Dorsey came in then dick came in and and  you know they simply couldn't seem to  get it together on the inside couldn't  get aligned on the inside and there's  always been a question about whether the  Twitter board has been completely  aligned because they're all of these  competing voices Emily with your  experience with Jack Dorsey what might  he bring to the company that is  different than dick costolo it might  help it so one of the things jack is is  is the product guy he's very much in  favor of simplicity and that's always  been his push he also is very supportive  of the live feed and keeping live as  sort of the the cornerstone of twitter  twitter has been experimenting with live  and delayed tweets as well to try to  help surface these tweets that happen  maybe eight hours ago that are really  cool but baby don't show up in your feed  because you only see what was written  five seconds ago and Jack Dorsey as i  understand it certainly cares about  keeping that chronological feed alive  but at the same time there's a question  of how do they make that easier for  people like my mom who's a huge facebook  user to get into twitter and to  understand Twitter without having to do  too much work figuring out who to follow  figuring out when to log on figuring out  user names I mean user names are very  difficult to understand on Twitter  search still leaves a lot to be desired  it's a very complicated product that  does have a lot of potential Emily you  know you know Wall Street as you said  has been really rough on Dick Costolo do  you think you know you say Jack's gonna  come back to be interim but in terms of  a long-term CEO do you expect that  they'll look outside the company or  other people inside the company that  could rise up and likely lead you know  be the new leadership so there are  certainly a lot of people inside the  company that have been talked about for  example Adam Bain has turned Twitter  into an incredible business I mean you  know the the questions about Twitter  have a lot more to do with user growth  than revenue growth and that's really  been Adam Adam Baynes you know project  he has turned Twitter into a revenue  engine Anthony nodo the CFO obviously  spent a long time at Goldman Sachs does  he have the sort of product sense the  product vision that Twitter needs some  people would question whether whether  indeed he does have that I mean one of  Twitter's main difficulties is that it  hasn't had a founder one of its founders  as a CEO and I was talking to Chris  Sacca about this very thing you know  does Dick Costolo have the respect of  the troops because he's not a founder  how much more difficult is it for him to  execute on his vision because it's not a  founder and he's not a founder and so I  could say it is difficult founder CEOs  you know regardless of whether they're  right or wrong people just defer to them  they're allowed to be weirder they're  allowed to take bigger risks and Dick  never had that that said there are names  being tossed around here in Silicon  Valley I've heard Kevin systems name the  founder of Instagram I've heard his name  talked around but at the same time I've  also heard a lot of people saying but  really who could really do this better I  mean they can't get Mark Zuckerberg but  it does certainly lend some more you  know some more you know more thought to  this idea that maybe Google or Facebook  comes in and buys Twitter you literally  stole the next question right out of my  mouth is that will Twitter net in  evitable East a an independent company  what would you wear would you please  place the odds on someone bigger like a  google or  facebook coming along and taking them  out I think historically Jack Dorsey  Evan Williams they have never wanted to  sell that this company Mark Zuckerberg  tried to buy this company several years  ago and they did not sell Chris Sacca  just told me this amazing anecdote about  being in Hawaii and getting the call and  listening on speakerphone as Evan Jack  and fizz went over the terms that Mark  Zuckerberg was offering them and he said  at that time there was never any doubt  that they wanted this to be a big  independent company that said you know  Mark Zuckerberg for example has shown no  fear of putting down a lot of money in  making big bets and Mark Zuckerberg at  least according to Chris Sacca would  love to own Twitter would love to  improve some of the things that he  thinks is wrong with Twitter I you know  who who knows a Twitter would certainly  add a lot of value to a company like  Google that has never gotten social  right but is it really going to happen  speculation about a twitter deal a big  twitter deal has been happening for  years and it's never happened

